# XBD 4.12 File Times Update
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Which operation marks which timestamp is stated on the page of each
# function and tested in its module (for example XSH.fstat.007, XSH.read.014,
# XSH.futimens.001 to .007, and the mkdir, link, unlink, rename and similar
# modules). The access time is marked by reading whatever the mount options
# are (XSH.read.014); these assertions are about the update rules
# themselves. Timestamps are compared with CLOCK_REALTIME; a step that
# needs the old value to be stale waits 1.3 s so that the resolution of up
# to a second cannot hide a missing update.

assertion  XBD.4.12-file-times-update.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.12
class      required
says       A newly created file has all three timestamps within a second of
           the clock when it was made (not later than the clock when it was
           looked at, not more than one second before it was made), valid
           nanosecond fields, and the directory it was made in has its
           modification and status change times likewise.
test       t/001-new-file-times.c

assertion  XBD.4.12-file-times-update.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.12
class      required
says       Updates marked by an operation are made by the time fstat(),
           stat() or lstat() reports them: after a write() (fstat), an
           ftruncate() that changes the size (stat) and a chmod() (lstat)
           the times that operation marks (modification and status change,
           status change) show the current time, within the resolution,
           even though the old value was more than 1.3 s older.
timeout    90
test       t/002-updates-before-stat.c

assertion  XBD.4.12-file-times-update.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.12
class      required
says       The timestamp resolution is no coarser than one second: two
           writes at least 1.2 s apart (by the monotonic clock) give
           different modification times and different status change times.
timeout    90
test       t/003-resolution.c

assertion  XBD.4.12-file-times-update.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.12
class      untestable
says       No marks or updates are made for files on a read-only file system.
reason     A test cannot make a read-only file system (mounting is not POSIX) and cannot rely on one being present; the read-only errors of the function pages are tested where a read-only descriptor or file exists.
